// HumanityOS ship machine layout: the machines that belong to the SHIP's shared zones (the
// Commons, the South Commons), not to any one home. Same format as data/machines/home.ron.
// docs/design/ship-homes-and-logistics.md (increment 1a) moved them out of home.ron.
//
// Positions are ZONE-LOCAL: offset x/z (and an array's origin) are metres from the min corner
// of the zone the row names (`zone: "commons"`, whose box starts at ship (65, 0, 20); `zone:
// "commons-2"`, the South Commons, at ship (77, 0, 770)), and y is up from that zone's deck. The
// engine adds the zone's origin and keeps the machine inside the zone's box. `room` names the
// labelled volume a row stands in (data/blueprints/ship_structure.ron: "mess-hall",
// "recreation", "mess-hall-2"), or the zone itself.
//
// How it is loaded: a household file that names this file (`ship_machines: Some("ship.ron")` in
// data/machines/home.ron and data/machines/cabin.ron) gets these rows merged in when it loads, and
// the editor writes them back here, not into the household file, and only in the Dev play mode.
// The family home names it because its battery bank powers the Commons' tent humidifier, CO2 fan
// and fish tank and its loops count the Commons garden: that is how the household was modelled
// before the split, and moving those machines onto the ship's own supply (and off the
// household's meters) is a later increment. Those three power wires are the family home's own
// rows (home.ron's connections, since increment 6 lane 1A): a wire from a household's machine
// stays with that household (src/machines.rs `split_for_save`), so a cabin, which merges this
// file too, finds no wire here naming a battery it does not have. home_solo.ron does not name
// this file, so the one-person home loads without the Commons, as before.
//
// THE CATALOG (increment 6, lane 1A) defines every type these rows use, so any household file
// can merge it: each copied from data/machines/home.ron, which defines the same types the same
// way (the test `a_type_two_machine_files_define_is_defined_the_same` in src/machines.rs holds
// every file to it), and the new serving_counter. A household file's own definition wins on a
// merge, and an editor save of a household file leaves out the types this file defines.
//
// THE MESS HALL'S FURNITURE (increment 6, plan section 3.3). The Commons' mess hall is its north
// strip, Commons-local x 0..30, z 0..8 (the volume "mess-hall"):
//   - the SERVING COUNTER along the north wall at its west end, x 0.4..2.8, z 0.3..0.9, its front
//     to the south: the ship's food store stands 1.1 m in front of it (data/food/ship_stores.ron,
//     spot (2, 2)), so E at the counter is E at the store;
//   - four DINING TABLES with four chairs each in a row along the north half, x 5..19, z 1.25 to
//     2.25 (chairs at z 0.75 and 2.75): the crew's maintenance hand walks z 4 between the stores,
//     the tables and the floor (data/npc/chores.ron), and every crew walk keeps 0.5 m from every
//     piece of furniture here (the relay test `every_crew_walk_is_clear_of_the_ships_furniture`);
//   - the crew's LONG TABLE, three dining tables end to end at x 29.55..30.55, z 1.4..7.4: east of
//     the crew's own meal seats (x 29, z 1.5 to 7.25), so a crew member sits on its west side,
//     facing east, and the walks from their work in the hall to their seats never cross it.
// THE RECREATION ROOM (the volume "recreation", Commons-local x 0.5..10.5, z 43.5..51.5, the
// Commons' south-west corner): a wall screen on the west wall facing east, a rug, a couch facing
// the screen and one along the rug's north side.
// THE SOUTH COMMONS (zone "commons-2", halfway down First Street): its mess hall ("mess-hall-2",
// its north strip, x 0..24, z 0..8) has its own serving counter, where the South Commons' store
// stands (spot (2, 2)), and three tables with their chairs. Furniture is drawn as its model's box
// where its model is missing: the serving counter is a plain box (no counter model is on disk;
// question 5 of the plan).

(
    catalog: {
        "aeroponic_tower_apothecary": (
            shape: "cylinder",
            size: (0.16, 1.9, 0.16),
            color: (0.45, 0.55, 0.3),
            label: "Apothecary tower",
            category: "Food",
            stats: [
                (
                    kind: "nutrient",
                    value: "herbs + teas",
                    status: "ok",
                ),
                (
                    kind: "power",
                    value: "sun-lit; a 35 W pump on 5 minutes in every 50: 3.5 W averaged",
                    status: "ok",
                ),
            ],
            // Its pump (2026-09-27; it had none, so the meter charged it nothing while its stat
            // said -12 W). Tower Garden's standard submersible pump, the maker's own figures (read
            // 2026-09-27): "This pump is rated at 357 US GPH, 0.65 amps, 35 watts @120 VAC/60 Hz"
            // (https://us.towergarden.com/products/pump), switched by the maker's timer, "Select I
            // for Indoor use (5 min on, 45 min off)" (https://us.towergarden.com/products/tower-garden-timer).
            // The towers stand indoors, so it runs a tenth of the time: 3.5 W averaged over a day.
            // A tower has no power role (the electrical sim does not run its pump, home_spawn.rs),
            // so its port is what the static meter charges; the port therefore carries that
            // continuous-equivalent 3.5 W, the convention home_solo.ron's washer and water heater
            // already use, and the 35 W nameplate lives in the label and the stat.
            power: None,
            ports: [
                (
                    utility: Electricity,
                    dir: In,
                    label: "pump (35 W, on 5 min in 50: 3.5 W averaged)",
                    watts: 3.5,
                    flow_lpm: 0.0,
                    mbps: 0.0,
                    anchor: (0.0, 0.0, 0.0),
                ),
            ],
            storage: [],
            auto_recipe: None,
            container_type: None,
            model: None,
        ),
        "aeroponic_tower_nutrition": (
            shape: "cylinder",
            size: (0.3, 2.4, 0.3),
            color: (0.3, 0.65, 0.38),
            label: "Variety tower",
            category: "Food",
            stats: [
                (
                    kind: "nutrient",
                    value: "vitamins + protein",
                    status: "ok",
                ),
                (
                    kind: "power",
                    value: "sun-lit; a 48 W pump on 5 minutes in every 50: 4.8 W averaged",
                    status: "ok",
                ),
            ],
            // Its pump (2026-09-27). The port was 15 W of "pump + light" for 24 hours, 0.36 kWh a
            // day a tower and 11.9 kWh for the 33 placed; the tower is sun-lit and has no light.
            // Tower Garden's high capacity submersible pump, the maker's own figures (read
            // 2026-09-27): "This pump is rated at 714 GPH, 0.43 amps, 48 watts @ 120 VAC/60 Hz"
            // (https://us.towergarden.com/products/pump-model-3-0). Which pump is a GAME CHOICE:
            // this tower is 2.4 m and 50 cups, taller than a standard Tower Garden, and its parts
            // list (data/towers/aeroponic_configs.ron) asks for a pump that lifts over 2.5 m, so it
            // gets the maker's larger pump (twice the standard 357 GPH, 35 W). The maker's timer:
            // "Select I for Indoor use (5 min on, 45 min off)"
            // (https://us.towergarden.com/products/tower-garden-timer), so it runs a tenth of the
            // time, 4.8 W averaged. A tower has no power role (the electrical sim does not run its
            // pump, home_spawn.rs), so its port is what the static meter charges; the port carries
            // that continuous-equivalent 4.8 W and the 48 W nameplate lives in the label and stat.
            power: None,
            ports: [
                (
                    utility: Electricity,
                    dir: In,
                    label: "pump (48 W, on 5 min in 50: 4.8 W averaged)",
                    watts: 4.8,
                    flow_lpm: 0.0,
                    mbps: 0.0,
                    anchor: (0.0, 0.0, 0.0),
                ),
            ],
            storage: [],
            auto_recipe: None,
            container_type: None,
            model: None,
        ),
        "aquaponic_tank": (
            shape: "box",
            size: (1.6, 1.0, 1.6),
            color: (0.2, 0.45, 0.7),
            label: "Aquaponic tank",
            category: "Food",
            stats: [
                (
                    kind: "food",
                    value: "+75 kcal/d (estimate, fish not modelled)",
                    status: "ok",
                ),
                (
                    kind: "nutrient",
                    value: "B12 + omega-3",
                    status: "ok",
                ),
                (
                    kind: "power",
                    value: "-17 W air pump, all day: 0.41 kWh/d",
                    status: "ok",
                ),
            ],
            // Its air pump (2026-09-27; the stat said 50 W, which no source backed, and it had no
            // power, so the meter charged nothing). How much air: FAO's Small-scale aquaponic food
            // production (Somerville et al. 2014, FAO Fisheries and Aquaculture Technical Paper
            // 589, https://www.fao.org/4/i4021e/i4021e.pdf, read 2026-09-27), section 7.3: "The
            // suggested rate is 5–8 litres of air per minute for each cubic metre of water,
            // coming from at least 2 air stones in different locations in the fish tank", so this
            // 1.6 x 1.0 x 1.6 m tank, 2.56 m3, wants 12.8 to 20.5 L a minute at the bottom of about
            // a metre of water. The pump: a HIBLOW HP-20 linear air pump, whose spec sheet
            // (https://www.hiblow-usa.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/02/HP-20-40_170119.pdf, read
            // 2026-09-27) gives "Rated Loading Pressure kPa 9.8" (a metre of water), "Airflow
            // Volume" 20 L/min at 60 Hz and "Power Consumption W 17": the top of FAO's range, for
            // 17 W. FAO wants the aeration on around the clock ("simple battery backups for air
            // pumps have saved countless fish"), so it is a steady load, priority 1, shed last.
            power: Some(Consumer(
                watts: 17.0,
                priority: 1,
            )),
            ports: [],
            storage: [],
            auto_recipe: None,
            container_type: None,
            model: None,
        ),
        "composter": (
            shape: "box",
